.TH SH-TILES 1
.SH NAME
sh-tiles \- build a screen of tiles from a shell script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load tiles
.PP
.B tiles new
.I WxH
[
.I scale
] [
.I title
]
.br
.B tiles
.I id
.IR command ...
.br
.B tiles
.I id
.B draw
.I surface
.br
.B tiles
.I id
.B write
.I surface file
.br
.B tiles del
.I id
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sh-tiles
gives shell scripts
.IR tiles (2)'s
own command language, the way
.IR sh-plot (1)
gives them
.IR plot (2)'s
and
.IR sh-tk (1)
gives them
.IR tk (2)'s.
.PP
What that is for:
.IR tiles (2)
draws from named palettes, named sheets of art and named surfaces, all
of them made by text, and its art and its levels are ordinary files.
Put those two together and a screenful of 8-bit graphics is something a
shell script can build \- which is the same trick that makes a Tk window
something a shell script can build, and for the same reason.
.PP
Nothing in this module knows what any of the commands mean.
Everything after the handle is forwarded verbatim to
.IR tiles (2)'s
.IR cmd ,
and only
.BR new ,
.BR del ,
.B draw
and
.B write
\- the window and the file, which
.IR tiles (2)
has no opinion about \- are special-cased first words.
A forwarded command's own failure is reported as a soft
.BR $status ,
which is
.IR sh-tk (1)'s
convention for a bad raw tk command; only handle and usage errors raise.
.PP
In
.B ${}
the answer is the value, so a script can ask a question:
.B ${tiles $id size level}
is
.BR "256 96" ,
and a command that fails hands back its
.B !
message rather than aborting the script.
.PP
.B Draw
puts a named surface in the window;
.B write
puts it in a file, as a line of
.B "W H BYTESPERPIXEL"
followed by the pixels, so a script can build a screen and keep it with
nobody looking at a window.
.SH EXAMPLE
A whole screen, with the art and the level in files and not one line of
Limbo:
.IP
.EX
load std
load tiles
id = ${tiles new 256x96 2 \(aqtiles from sh\(aq}
tiles $id palette turf  dark.black dark.green  mid.lime   light.lime
tiles $id palette brick dark.black dark.orange mid.orange light.orange
tiles $id palette hud   dark.black pale.grey   pale.grey  pale.grey
tiles $id sheet art /lib/tiles/demo.chr
tiles $id font font
tiles $id surface level 256 96 dark.black
tiles $id map level art /lib/tiles/demo.map
tiles $id text level 8 4 hud BUILT BY SH
tiles $id draw level
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/tiles.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-plot (1),
.IR sh-tk (1),
.IR tiles (2),
.IR tilestest (1)
.SH BUGS
.B del
forgets the handle and leaves the window on screen, because
.I tkclient->wmctl
with
.B exit
would exit the whole process group and this module is a guest in
whatever process runs the script.
.IR Sh-plot (1)
and
.IR sh-tk (1)
make the same trade for the same reason.
.PP
There is no way to hand a surface back to a Limbo program from here, so
a script can build a screen and draw it or write it, and cannot pass it
to something else.
.PP
Nothing here reads the keyboard.
A script can build a screen; it cannot yet play one.
